# LIGHT desktop package set — Orange Pi 5 / Orange Pi 5 Plus (Rockchip RK3588/
# RK3588S, arm64)
#
# A LEAN desktop, NOT the full Plasma+gaming stage4. Appended to packages.list
# by build-orangepi5-stage4.sh UNCONDITIONALLY (this platform has ONE role — no
# --desktop light|none toggle the way Frite has one; every image built here is
# the light desktop). Sized for the Mali-G610 (Panthor kernel driver / Mesa
# "panfrost" gallium driver — see make.conf.tmpl): X11 + a lightweight WM + a
# panel + a file manager + a terminal + a minimal browser. No KDE/GNOME/Plasma,
# no compositor-heavy Wayland session, no gaming stack. Mirrors
# specs-frite/portage/packages-light-desktop.list and
# specs-rpi5/portage/packages-desktop.list content 1:1 (same lean-LXDE recipe).

# ── Display server (X11; lean) ─────────────────────────────────────
x11-base/xorg-server
# Materializes the X drivers from INPUT_DEVICES=libinput + VIDEO_CARDS=panfrost
# (already unconditional in specs-orangepi5/portage/make.conf.tmpl); without it
# Xorg has no input/video driver and the greeter is unusable.
x11-base/xorg-drivers
x11-apps/xrandr
x11-apps/xset
x11-apps/setxkbmap
x11-terms/xterm

# ── Desktop session: LXDE-lite (PCManFM + Openbox + LXpanel) ───────
# LXDE is the canonical "lean desktop": GTK-based, no compositor required, runs
# comfortably on any of the RAM SKUs these boards ship (4/8/16/32GB). Openbox is
# the WM; lxpanel the panel; pcmanfm the file manager; lxsession ties the
# session together.
x11-wm/openbox
lxde-base/lxpanel
lxde-base/lxsession
x11-misc/pcmanfm
lxde-base/lxappearance

# ── Login / session bring-up ───────────────────────────────────────
# LightDM is the lean display manager; on a single-user appliance you can also
# autostart Openbox from .xinitrc and drop lightdm — see RUNNING.md.
x11-misc/lightdm
x11-misc/lightdm-gtk-greeter
# The OpenRC `display-manager` init script does NOT ship with lightdm itself —
# it is provided by gui-libs/display-manager-init (configured via
# /etc/conf.d/display-manager DISPLAYMANAGER="lightdm", written by the
# builder). Without this the builder would enable a service the image never
# provides and the light-desktop image would boot to a bare console — this is
# the EXACT bug RPi5's Phase 3 review caught (missing lightdm.conf -> blank
# greeter); get it right from the start here (see build-orangepi5-stage4.sh).
gui-libs/display-manager-init

# ── Desktop-governance runtime (SSO `desktop-sudo` claim -> polkit) ─────
# The SAME runtime specs-x86/portage/packages-desktop.list declares under
# "SSO-claim sudo runtime" (full rationale there + docs/CLIENT-IMAGES.md).
# keys-provision's provision_desktop_governance() is shared by every image:
# on a GOVERNED client (templates baked or on the KEYS USB) it renders the
# default-deny polkit rules.d gate and the stdlib-only python mapper cron, and
# strips sudoers/%wheel — so polkit becomes the ONLY escalation route. Both
# runtimes are declared here so that route is a stated contract of this list,
# not an accident of the dependency graph:
#   sys-auth/polkit  — today arrives only through lxde-base/lxsession's
#                      COMMON_DEPEND; a session-manager swap must not silently
#                      take the governed image's sole sudo path with it. It must
#                      be built with elogind session tracking (the ebuild's
#                      ConsoleKit fallback sees no sessions) — the global USE=elogind in make.conf.tmpl.
#   dev-lang/python  — the mapper's interpreter; present through portage anyway,
#                      declared for the same reason as on x86.
# The mapper's 60s floor engine (sys-process/cronie) is in packages.list and in
# the builder's DEFAULT_SVCS. Pinned by tests/scripts/test-arm-desktop-package-sets.sh.
sys-auth/polkit
dev-lang/python

# DELIBERATELY NOT gnome-extra/nm-applet (the x86 list's graphical NM front-end):
# its hard RDEPEND net-libs/libnma needs gui-libs/gtk-4 UNCONDITIONALLY — the
# exact chain the per-board package.use already pins libfm[-automount] and
# pinentry[-gtk] off for, because gtk-4 fails to cross-configure — and its
# +modemmanager default USE-requires networkmanager[modemmanager], which every
# ARM spec forces off. Network configuration on this desktop is nmtui/nmcli
# (networkmanager[+tools]) from x11-terms/xterm. Tracked as a separate issue.

# ── Minimal apps ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
media-gfx/feh
app-editors/nano
# No browser is REQUESTED here. www-client/netsurf used to be listed, but this
# board's profile/package.provided also lists it (its duktape binding generator
# needs net-libs/nsgenbind as a native build tool the cross host lacks), so portage
# never merges it ("will not be merged because it is listed in package.provided")
# and the requested-atom completeness gate (scripts/_payload-gate-lib.sh) then
# failed EVERY desktop build of this board on a package nobody could build. The
# image never shipped a browser; the list now says so. Re-add www-client/netsurf
# here only together with removing it from package.provided and a working
# cross build of it (prover B, La Frite build 2026-11-10/11).

# ── Fonts (so the lean desktop is legible out of the box) ──────────
media-fonts/dejavu
media-fonts/noto

# ── Emulated-desktop display agent (QEMU runner --display spice) ──────────────
# When the containerised QEMU runner opens this image with a SPICE display, the
# native viewer (remote-viewer/spicy) resizes the GUEST to its window. The agent
# gives that session agent-mouse, clipboard and the XRandR path, and the
# session-side applier (/usr/libexec/hugmesh/vm-autofit, xdg autostart) applies
# the new preferred mode on a virtio-gpu/qxl hotplug — the lean LXDE session has
# no output daemon of its own. Both are inert on real hardware: the daemon is
# started by /etc/local.d/20-spice-vdagent.start ONLY when the SPICE virtio port
# exists (the stock OpenRC script fails red without it), and the applier exits
# at once unless the DRM driver is virtio_gpu or qxl.
#   app-emulation/spice-vdagent  — 0.22.1; USE=selinux (global) pulls the policy
#                                  module below, which the enforcing image loads
#                                  with every other installed .pp.
#   sec-policy/selinux-vdagent   — listed EXPLICITLY so the requested-atom
#                                  completeness gate asserts it installed; it is
#                                  stable on amd64 AND arm64 (2.20260616_p1), so
#                                  it needs NO keyword entry — and must not get
#                                  one (see the ARM keyword files).
app-emulation/spice-vdagent
sec-policy/selinux-vdagent
